#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16104c2f-285a-58e3-a912-1c5c32735793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269131} {:answer/id #uuid "d7796c45-683b-5992-8c9f-34c50eb36c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269132} {:answer/id #uuid "551cfa18-cedc-5bbe-8306-3487d020644c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{112}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269133} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea035f0-6207-54f9-8514-fc252b0a6a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269134} {:answer/id #uuid "48b4dd83-fc3a-5279-9506-b1c1c610adae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7796c45-683b-5992-8c9f-34c50eb36c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269132} :answer-field/id #uuid "0865b498-b4a5-5b7f-ba31-8063a63b2589" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26ec9194-f492-518e-8975-e0a3b197c76c" :question/math-academy-id "q-19107" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,1,-1)$, $B(3,-2,2)$, and $C(-1,-2,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-2,2⟩,\\,c=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046481/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}})≈{54}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
